75, each guild with its own trading rights.","people":[]},{"n":5,"lv":5,"band":"commoners","name":"Townspeople","term":"meshchane, tsekhovye","lbl":"No reliable count","w":null,"firm":1,"pic":"","you":"You are registered in your town as one of its meshchane, keep a small shop or work at a craft, pay the poll tax and may be taken as a recruit.","kid":"Townspeople ran small shops and workshops in Russia’s towns and paid taxes to the government.","rise":"A townsman who gathered enough capital could enrol as a merchant, and peasants allowed to settle in a town could join the meshchane if they owned property and worked there.","why":"The meshchane were the lower urban estate, ranked above the peasants and below the merchant guilds: small traders, artisans, shopkeepers and urban workers who were registered in a town but could not meet the merchants’…","people":[]},{"n":6,"lv":5,"band":"specialists","name":"Cossacks","term":"kazaki, kazachye voisko","lbl":"No reliable count","w":null,"firm":0,"pic":"/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/matvei-platov.jpg","you":"You hold land free of taxes in your Cossack village, and in return you serve for years in your host’s regiments with your own horse and equipment.","kid":"Cossacks were horsemen of the frontier who were given land in return for serving in the army.","rise":"Cossack status passed from father to son, and the officers of the hosts could be admitted to the nobility.","why":"The Cossacks, once free frontier communities of runaway serfs, adventurers and steppe warriors, had become a hereditary military estate of the empire by the end of the 18th century.","people":[["matvei-platov","Matvei Platov"]]},{"n":7,"lv":6,"band":"specialists","name":"Soldiers","term":"soldaty, rekruty, nizhnie chiny","lbl":"1.5–2%","w":1.75,"firm":0,"pic":"","you":"You were chosen as a recruit by your village or your master and serve for twenty years or more, far from home; if you survive, you will come home a free man.","kid":"Russian soldiers were taken from their villages and served in the army for many years.","rise":"A few soldiers of humble birth rose to officer’s rank, which brought nobility under the Table of Ranks.","why":"The army’s rank and file were recruits drawn from the estates that paid the poll tax, above all the peasants.","people":[]},{"n":8,"lv":6,"band":"commoners","name":"State and crown peasants","term":"gosudarstvennye krestyane, udelnye krestyane","lbl":"43–44%","w":43.5,"firm":1,"pic":"","you":"You farm your commune’s share of state land, pay the poll tax and your quit-rent to the treasury and send recruits to the army; no one can sell you, but you cannot move away freely.","kid":"State peasants farmed land that belonged to the government, and unlike serfs they could not be sold.","rise":"A state peasant who prospered could buy land and could leave for a town or a merchant guild if he met their requirements; in the 18th century, however, the crown had given hundreds of thousands of state peasants to nobles as serfs.","why":"About as many peasants lived on the land of the state as on private estates.","people":[]},{"n":9,"lv":7,"band":"dependent","name":"Serfs","term":"krepostnye krestyane, pomeshchichi krestyane","lbl":"35–36%","w":35.5,"firm":1,"pic":"","you":"You work your lord’s fields three days a week or pay him a quit-rent, and he can punish you, sell you, send you to the army and must approve your marriage.","kid":"Serfs were farmers who belonged to a landowner and were not allowed to leave his estate.","rise":"A serf could be freed by his master, buy his freedom if the master agreed, or leave serfdom as a recruit. From 1803 lords could free whole villages as “free agriculturalists”, but by 1858 only about 152,000 male serfs, 1.5 per cent, had been freed that way.","why":"Serfs were peasants owned, with the land they lived on, by noble landlords, and bound to it since the Law Code of 1649.","people":[]},{"n":10,"lv":7,"band":"dependent","name":"Household serfs","term":"dvorovye lyudi","lbl":"2–3%","w":2.5,"firm":1,"pic":"","you":"You live in your master’s house as a cook, a groom or a maid, with no land of your own; he can sell you on your own, and his favour or cruelty rules your life.","kid":"Household serfs worked as servants in a landowner’s house and had no land of their own.","rise":"Freedom came by an owner’s grace or by purchase: Count Nikolai Sheremetev freed Praskovia Zhemchugova in 1798 and married her in secret in 1801.","why":"Beside the field serfs lived the household serfs (dvorovye), who had no land and served in their master’s house and stables as cooks, coachmen, grooms, maids, footmen, nurses and stewards.","people":[]}]}</script>
